   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The function f is defined by \\( f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 + x - 6}{x^2 - 4} \\) for \\( x \\neq 2 \\) and \\( f(2) = 7 \\). What is \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) \\)?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         5,
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "For x near 2 but not equal to 2, f(x) is given by the quotient. At x = 2 its numerator is 4 + 2 - 6 = 0 and its denominator is 4 - 4 = 0, so the form is zero over zero. The value f(2) = 7 plays no part in the limit.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Factor both parts: x^2 + x - 6 = (x - 2)(x + 3) and x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Subtract",
                  "x",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  3
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Subtract",
                  "x",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  2
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "For x not equal to 2 the common factor x - 2 divides out, leaving (x + 3)/(x + 2).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               3
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The simplified quotient is continuous at 2, so the limit is (2 + 3)/(2 + 2) = 5/4.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            5,
            4
         ]
      }
   ],
